  - path: docs/use-cases/web-ui.html
    tier: T2
    action: updated
    distills: "UC-6 becomes the round trip rather than the export alone: the heading, the doc's summary line and the header attribution all widen, and a new 'Bringing one back' subsection (id uc-6-import) carries the import half. Three request/answer rows - a HAR 1.2 document posted to POST /flows/import yielding {imported: n} with the entries ADDING to the capture (replacing is a clear followed by an import, not a mode hidden inside a POST); a malformed or hostile document refused 400 with a reason, an entry-level fault naming its index, and all-or-nothing so a refused import changes nothing at all; and an empty entry list as a 200 reporting zero, the same reading an empty export has. Plus the three consequences a reader cannot infer from the endpoint signature: fresh ids over original instants (so an imported capture sorts by when it happened, and the same file twice yields two independent sets carrying the same instants); a body the file does not carry coming back as an explicit not-captured rather than empty, including the foreign-writer case where calling it empty would assert a 4MB response was a 204; and what the format cannot carry being dropped rather than invented, with a body's bytes outranking a size that disagrees because the ring weighs what it actually holds. States up front that an imported flow is otherwise an ORDINARY flow - the same detail endpoint, the same body endpoints and their ?decode=1, the same /stream announcement, and the same ring rules, so a large import can push a live capture out. UC-3/UC-4's shared listing contract gained a ?source= row (every row carries its source, because the ring now holds flows the proxy never saw and a listing that mixes a live capture with a colleague's file has to say which is which) and its refusal row now names a bad source alongside a bad limit and cursor, adding that a silently empty listing reads exactly like 'nothing matched'. The provenance callout now attributes the export to W2 and the import plus the mark to W3, records that both directions live in one module so they cannot drift, and that round-trip fidelity and the never-panics claim are proven as properties rather than by example. Doc header to v6."
  - path: docs/decisions/dec-31-imported-flows-are-marked.html
    tier: T2
    action: added
    distills: "The epic plan's second planned decision, written in the WU where provenance became real. Records why an imported flow is a first-class flow carrying a closed-enum source in the same byte-weighted ring rather than a second collection, with the three rejected alternatives (a second store, which would double every read path in the API and make 'an imported flow is otherwise an ordinary flow' a claim the code contradicts; no mark at all, which leaves a user unable to see which rows are theirs or clear the file's rows without clearing their own; and a free-text source, whose filter is a substring match with no closed set to validate against, so a typo silently returns nothing). A diagram shows provenance as a property of a flow rather than a place it is stored, with both producers feeding one ring and every read path and the eviction rule applying to both kinds unchanged. States what the one-ring choice COSTS rather than hiding it: an import can evict a live capture, and the alternative (an import that cannot be evicted) would let a file permanently consume the memory the live capture needs; an imported flow takes a fresh handle over its original instant, so the same file twice is two copies in the ring rather than one row seen twice; the mark is on the summary row and not only the detail view; and an unrecognised ?source= is refused rather than ignored. A section on the CORS widening states it as an EXTENSION of DEC-12 rather than a reversal: both of DEC-12's grounds still hold - the bind address is unchanged and POST /flows/import is as reachable with curl as GET /flows always was - so the widening grants a webview exactly the access an already-local caller has, and what would reverse DEC-12 is binding beyond loopback or exposing something curl cannot reach, neither of which is on the table; without it the app's import control would be dead on arrival, since a webview's fetch cannot POST to an origin whose grant omits the method. A section on the all-or-nothing refusal traces each property to the SHAPE that guarantees it rather than to care: no partial state to roll back because nothing was inserted (a cleanup path could be forgotten, a type signature cannot); one bad entry refusing the document with its index, because a partial success is unrecoverable; the never-panics claim as a property over arbitrary bytes and near-miss documents; and the bytes outranking a declared size, because trusting the size would put a client's arithmetic in charge of Trawler's memory accounting. Five consequences bound to where they bind, including that a curl paste is a second format behind the same endpoint and the same mark rather than a third provenance, and that an imported flow is inspectable but never executable. The field mapping is code and is never copied here."
  - path: docs/decisions/dec-30-har-as-the-interchange-format.html
    tier: T2
    action: updated
    distills: "Amended where only the read side settles a fact. Its 'the importer is defined against this projection' consequence stops being a forward reference to W3 and names where the parser landed (src/api/har.rs), adding that it reads the reason tokens rather than re-spelling them. Its round-trip 'not preserved' list gained the two facts only importing reveals: a foreign writer's body with no text but a size claiming one existed comes back as an explicit not-captured, because reading it as empty would assert a 4MB response was a 204 - Trawler's own four states round-trip exactly, and a foreign tool's 'there was a body, I don't have it' collapses to the one state that says exactly that; and a request body Trawler never captured, where HAR's postData has no shape for it so the export omits postData and carries the fact in bodySize alone, which means every uncaptured state on the request leg collapses to the coarsest honest one while the response leg (where every capture-path skip actually occurs) keeps the full vocabulary. A new callout records that on the way back in a body's bytes outrank a size that disagrees with them: such files are accepted rather than refused because real tools emit them, but the ring is weighted by retained bytes and letting a client's arithmetic set that weight would put eviction under the file's control. Header meta now reads 'E-EXPORT.W2, amended by E-EXPORT.W3'."
